artifact.self_contained_problem:
In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, the azimuthal distribution of produced particles in a given event can be expanded in a Fourier series, and the harmonic flow coefficients v_n are extracted from two-particle correlations. Define the two-particle Fourier coefficient V_{nΔ}(p_T^a, p_T^t) for trigger transverse momentum p_T^t and associated transverse momentum p_T^a as the n-th Fourier coefficient of the pair azimuthal distribution; one says that V_{nΔ} factorizes if V_{nΔ}(p_T^a, p_T^t) = v_n(p_T^a) v_n(p_T^t). Let Ψ_m denote the m-th-order event plane, and define v_n{Ψ_m} = ⟨cos(n(φ − Ψ_m))⟩, the n-th harmonic flow coefficient measured with respect to Ψ_m.

Devise and analyze a concrete program of measurements and observables that:

(a) tests factorization of V_{nΔ} as a function of (p_T^a, p_T^t) over a wide kinematic range, in order to separate collective bulk anisotropies from non-flow contributions and to characterize event-by-event flow fluctuations;

(b) extends factorization tests to the space of trigger and associated pseudorapidity (η^a, η^t), formulating the appropriate two-particle observable V_{nΔ}(η^a, η^t) and identifying the signatures that would distinguish flow from non-flow in pseudorapidity;

(c) uses v_n{Ψ_m} with m ≠ n (mixed-harmonic event-plane correlations) to probe the hydrodynamic response and quantify mode mixing between initial-state anisotropies ε_m and final-state harmonics v_n.

Specify the observables, their expected behavior under pure flow versus non-flow scenarios, and a procedure for using them to disentangle bulk anisotropies, flow fluctuations, and hydrodynamic mode mixing.

